hey everyone it's sarah with registered  nurse orange comm and in this video  we're going to go over the scale of how  to place electrodes for a 12-lead EKG  now before you get started you need to  get your supplies and what you need for  supplies is some electrodes you will  need ten of them and electrodes look  like this and they have a backing on the  back you will need some alcohol prep to  clean the skin and remove oils you also  need some exfoliation paper  I call it sandpaper on that looks like  tape because you're going to rough the  top layer of the skin up so what you do  is you clean your landmark areas and  then with alcohol prep and then you take  exfoliation paper and you lightly go  back and forth just to rough up that top  layer of skin and then you place the  electrode in the place where it needs to  go now if you have a patient who has a  lot of hair on their chest on the  landmarks of where your EKG stickers  need to go you need to trim those hairs  because it will cause artifact and also  be sure to watch out for any pacemakers  sub-q ports anything like that because  you'll want to remove the you'll not  want to put a sticker over those places  now some key things to remember about  your electrode make sure that they are  not expired because if they are expired  and you will get a bad connection and  also a lot of times I've had whenever  I've removed the backing of the  electrode off the gel that's on the  electrode was really dry and that caused  artifact and I didn't get a good  connection so make sure that they're  stored properly and that they're not  expired so let me demonstrate for you on  a mannequin how to place these 12-lead  EKG and what landmarks you use okay we  have our mannequin and what we're going  to do is you need to prep the chest  first so what you're going to do is take  your alcohol prep and clean your  landmarked areas and then you'll take  your exfoliation paper and just barely  scrape the skin on your landmarks areas  which will go  here in a second and remember if this  patient had a hairy chest you would want  to trim the hair on the chest you want  to avoid going over any implanted  devices like sub-q pores pacemakers icds  anything like that and if they have a  medication patch you don't want to go  over the medication patch so first what  we're going to do is we're going to  place our arm leads now you can use your  left right arms and lower legs but you  can also use the chest and since this  thumbing doesn't have in the arms or  legs we're going to use the chest so  first what we're going to place we're  going to place our right arm lis and  you're going to find the clavicle and  you're just going to go right below the  clavicle so we've prepped in everything  so what we're going to do is we're going  to take the backing off of the electrode  make sure these are not dry make sure  they're nice and lubricated because if  they're dry you'll get artifact and  you're just going to put it on the skin  and make sure it's nice and flesh on it  then we're going to go to the left arm  lead and we're going to find our  clavicle and we're just going to go  right below it make sure you're not  going over in any implanted devices  because that's really where they like to  be we're going to we've already prepped  so we're just going to place that over  there now let's go to our lower arm  leads we're going to go to the right  lower leg and for that you can go in the  upper right area of the quadrant so here  on this mannequin it is right here  so we're just going to place that there  and then we're going to go to the left  arm lead so we're going to go in the  left upper quadrant for that area as  well now let's do our chest leads first  what we're going to start with is v1  you're going to find the sternum which  is in the middle then you're going to  count the ribs and we're going to go to  the fourth intercostal space right of  the sternum so you're going to count as  ribs so about right here would be our  fourth intercostal space and that is  going to be v1 now let's place v2  we're going to go left to the sternum  and we're going to go to the fourth  intercostal space and it should be right  beside over to the left of where  everyone is so v2 will be right here and  we're going to skip v4  I mean v3 and we're going to go to v4  because v3 will be placed in between v2  and v4 so v4 is the fifth intercostal  space midclavicular so you're going to  find the clavicle the middle of the  clavicle and you're going to go down to  the fifth intercostal space which will  be right here and that's going to be v4  and now we're ready for v3 and this is  midway between v2 and v4 so v3 will be  right here and we're going to place that  here and now we're going to skip to b6  and we'll come back to v5 and these six  will be right here and it is going to be  mid axillary and it's going to be the  same level as v4 which is right here so  you're going to find the armpit and  you're going to go in between in the  middle of where the armpit is so about  right here on the patient lining it up  with v4 so right here is where it'd be  six to beat and now we're going to go to  b5 and this is the anterior axillary  line so it's not mid-axillary line like  v6 its anterior absolute axillary line  and it's the same level as v4 and v6 so  it will go in between that okay and now  you were done you have your right arm  left arm right leg left leg you have v1  v2 v3 v4 v5 and v6 so that is how you  place lectroids  for a 12-lead  Keiji okay so that is how you place 12  lead EKG electrodes on a patient be sure  to check out my other nursing skill  videos and subscribe to this YouTube  channel thank you so much for watching
 

